  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1032 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nA few storms linger into the overnight as outflows stagnate,\nfocusing in Otero County. Mainly dry conditions prevail through \ntomorrow morning. The monsoon high drifts eastward for Friday over\ncentral NM, keeping moisture levels slightly above normal for \nlate August. PWs around 1.1\" will allow for scattered convection \nto develop over area mtns and isolated activity for the lowlands \nin the afternoon, similar to what we saw today (storm motion to \nSW). Instability will be comparable to Thu with outflow gusts to \n40 mph possible. The Sacs are favored to see heavy rainfall with \nup to 2\" of rain in spots from the stronger cells according to the\nHREF. The activity should decay around sunset.\n\nThe upper high is pushed over the southern Plains this weekend by a \ntrough moving through the northern Rockies. Drier air from the east \nwill move in as a result with storm chances favoring AZ and western \nNM. Most areas should be dry this weekend as PWs fall to 0.9-1\" at \nKELP. PWs hold closer to 1.1\" along the AZ-NM line with a marginal \nthreat of heavy rainfall with storm motion to the N.\n\nThe forecast becomes more uncertain beginning on Mon as high \npressure aloft stretches from the southern Plains to the Carolinas \nin kind of a kidney bean shape. The LREF mean shows PWs rebounding \nto near normal from Mon onward, signifying better storm coverage \neach afternoon (isolated lowlands and scattered mtns). Inverted \ntroughs may move into our region from the Gulf, but we'll have to \nsee what features develop and exactly where the high sets up. For \nnow, typical monsoon activity is forecast for the second half of the \nperiod with gusty winds and brief downpours possible. Temps will be \na few degrees above normal through the middle of next week, a bit \ncooler than we were the past few days.",
